      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, on Earth, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the fundamental elixir of life, a dynamic shaping force of nature, and one of the most unique substances in the universe. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is special, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical States (The Three Phases)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, which fills our oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F), found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F), existing invisibly in the air as humidity or visibly as clouds and steam.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nScientists consider water a \"weird\" molecule because it defies many standard rules of chemistry. These quirks make life possible:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its electrical polarity (one side is slightly positive, the other slightly negative), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients and how plants absorb minerals from the soil.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a massive amount of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps living bodies from overheating.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense as ice. Because ice floats, lakes and oceans freeze from the top down, insulating the liquid water underneath so marine life can survive the winter.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which allows water to travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth"
